insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('sc-f-500','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$What is the difference between a Cognito user pool and a Cognito identity pool?$$,$$A user pool is a directory that authenticates end users directly (sign-up, sign-in, its own JWTs); an identity pool (federated identities) exchanges a user pool token, or a third-party IdP token, for temporary AWS credentials via STS so the app can call AWS services directly.$$),
('sc-f-501','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$What does STS AssumeRoleWithSAML do?$$,$$It exchanges a SAML 2.0 assertion from a corporate identity provider (AD FS, Okta, Entra ID) for temporary AWS credentials, letting a workforce user federate into an IAM role without ever having an IAM user.$$),
('sc-f-502','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$What does STS AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity do?$$,$$It exchanges an OIDC token from a public web identity provider, such as Google or Cognito, for temporary AWS credentials - the mechanism behind mobile and web app federation, and behind IAM roles for Kubernetes service accounts.$$),
('sc-f-503','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$Why require a unique ExternalId when a third-party vendor assumes a cross-account role on your behalf?$$,$$It closes the confused deputy problem: without a distinct, secret ExternalId condition in the trust policy, a multi-tenant vendor's platform could be manipulated into using the role meant for a different customer. Each customer gets a different ExternalId tied only to their own role.$$),
('sc-f-504','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$When would you use STS GetSessionToken instead of AssumeRole?$$,$$GetSessionToken issues temporary credentials for the calling IAM identity itself - same permissions, optionally MFA-protected - with no change of role. AssumeRole instead switches to a different role's permissions, typically for cross-account access or federation.$$),
('sc-f-505','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$What problem does IAM Roles Anywhere solve?$$,$$It lets servers or workloads running outside AWS, such as on-premises, obtain temporary IAM role credentials by presenting an X.509 certificate from a trusted private certificate authority, avoiding long-term access keys for hybrid workloads.$$),
('sc-f-506','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$How do you preserve an individual's identity in CloudTrail when they role-chain through a shared IAM role?$$,$$Require an sts:SourceIdentity condition in the trust policy and have each user set their own source identity on the first AssumeRole call. AWS propagates that source identity through every later role in the chain, so downstream CloudTrail events still show who really started it.$$),
('sc-f-507','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$What is a permission set in AWS IAM Identity Center?$$,$$A template of one or more IAM policies that IAM Identity Center provisions as an IAM role in a target account when an assigned user or group signs in - it is what actually defines that account's access, on top of whatever identity source and federation brought the user in.$$),
('sc-a-160','awsscs','acronym',4,'4.1',$$MFA$$,$$Multi-Factor Authentication - proving identity with a second factor (a virtual or hardware TOTP token, or a FIDO security key) in addition to a password.$$),
('sc-a-161','awsscs','acronym',4,'4.1',$$SCIM$$,$$System for Cross-domain Identity Management - the protocol IAM Identity Center uses to automatically sync users and groups from an external identity provider.$$),
('sc-a-162','awsscs','acronym',4,'4.1',$$OIDC$$,$$OpenID Connect - an identity layer on top of OAuth 2.0 used for web identity federation, such as STS AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity.$$),
('sc-y-080','awsscs','feynman',4,'4.1',$$Explain when you would use AssumeRoleWithSAML, AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity, and GetSessionToken.$$,$$Model answer: All three come out of STS as temporary credentials, but they answer different "who is asking" questions. AssumeRoleWithSAML is for an enterprise workforce that already authenticates against a corporate SAML 2.0 identity provider like AD FS or Okta - the SAML assertion proves who they are, and the call trades it for a role. AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity is the same idea for a public OIDC provider instead, like a mobile app's "sign in with Google" flow or a Kubernetes pod's service-account token - an OIDC token comes in, role credentials go out. GetSessionToken is different in kind: it doesn't change identity or role at all, it just re-issues temporary credentials for the same IAM user (or root) who's already calling, most commonly so an MFA code can be attached to satisfy an aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent condition on a later call. So the choice is: workforce SAML federation, app/service OIDC federation, or "make my own session MFA-aware" - three different problems, three different STS calls.$$),
('sc-f-508','awsscs','core',4,'4.2',$$What does the iam:PassRole permission control?$$,$$It governs which IAM roles a principal is allowed to hand to an AWS service when creating a resource - such as attaching an instance role while launching EC2, or an execution role while creating a Lambda function. Scoping PassRole to a specific list of role ARNs stops a low-privileged user from attaching a role more powerful than their own and escalating.$$),
('sc-f-509','awsscs','core',4,'4.2',$$How do you let an S3 bucket trigger a Lambda function on object upload?$$,$$Attach a resource-based policy to the Lambda function that grants the s3.amazonaws.com service principal permission to invoke it for that bucket. The function's execution role is a separate, identity-based policy that controls what the function itself can call outward - it doesn't control who may invoke the function.$$),
('sc-f-510','awsscs','core',4,'4.2',$$How do SCPs at different levels of an AWS Organizations OU hierarchy combine?$$,$$SCPs are inherited down the OU tree and intersected at every level - an account's effective permissions are capped by its own SCP, every SCP on each parent OU above it, and the root. An explicit deny at any level in that chain, even a distant parent OU, cannot be overridden by an allow lower down.$$),
('sc-f-511','awsscs','core',4,'4.2',$$Besides finding external access, what does IAM Access Analyzer's policy validation check?$$,$$At authoring time, it checks a policy document for syntax errors, security warnings such as an overly broad wildcard, and best-practice suggestions - catching problems before the policy is ever deployed, independent of the external-access findings feature.$$),
('sc-f-512','awsscs','core',4,'4.2',$$What does the aws:SourceVpce condition key restrict?$$,$$It limits access to requests that arrived through a specific VPC endpoint, so a policy can deny traffic even from IP addresses inside the VPC if it didn't pass through that endpoint - a tighter control than filtering by aws:SourceIp alone.$$),
('sc-a-163','awsscs','acronym',4,'4.2',$$ARN$$,$$Amazon Resource Name - the globally unique identifier for an AWS resource, used to scope the Resource element in IAM and resource-based policy statements.$$),
('sc-a-164','awsscs','acronym',4,'4.2',$$RBAC$$,$$Role-Based Access Control - permissions assigned through a fixed set of roles or groups, contrasted with attribute-based access control, which evaluates dynamic tags instead.$$),
('sc-y-081','awsscs','feynman',4,'4.2',$$Explain why an explicit Deny in a higher-level SCP always wins even when a lower-level identity policy explicitly allows the same action.$$,$$Model answer: Think of every layer, from the Organizations root down through each OU to the account and finally the identity policy, as a filter the request must pass through, not a vote it can win by majority. Each SCP in that chain can only take permissions away - it can restrict what's already allowed, never grant something new - so effective permission at any point is the intersection of everything above it. An identity policy's Allow only matters for actions that already survived every SCP above the account. If any one of those SCPs contains an explicit Deny for the action, that action never survives to reach the identity policy at all, no matter how clearly the identity policy allows it - there's nothing left to intersect with. That's why security teams put irreversible guardrails, like "never delete CloudTrail," in an SCP at a high OU: no account admin below it can grant their way around it.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('sc-q-500','awsscs',4,'4.1','mcq',1,$q$A mobile app lets end users sign in with a social identity provider and then needs to call Amazon S3 directly using temporary AWS credentials scoped to that individual user. Which Amazon Cognito component issues those temporary AWS credentials?$q$,$q$["A Cognito user pool","A Cognito identity pool (federated identities)","An IAM Identity Center permission set","An IAM Access Analyzer archive rule"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A Cognito identity pool exchanges a signed-in user's token for temporary, scoped AWS credentials via STS so the app can call AWS services directly. A user pool only handles authentication (sign-up/sign-in) and issues its own tokens, not AWS credentials; IAM Identity Center targets workforce access to accounts, not app end users; an Access Analyzer archive rule suppresses findings and grants nothing.$q$),
('sc-q-501','awsscs',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$A workforce authenticates against an on-premises Active Directory Federation Services SAML 2.0 identity provider and needs temporary AWS credentials to sign in to the AWS Management Console. Which STS API call accomplishes this federation?$q$,$q$["AssumeRoleWithSAML","AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity","GetSessionToken","GetFederationToken"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$AssumeRoleWithSAML exchanges a SAML 2.0 assertion from a corporate IdP like AD FS for temporary role credentials. AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity is for OIDC/web identity providers instead; GetSessionToken re-issues credentials for the calling IAM identity itself with no federation; GetFederationToken is a legacy API for granting a federated user limited permissions without SAML.$q$),
('sc-q-502','awsscs',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$A third-party monitoring vendor asks a company to create a cross-account IAM role that the vendor's platform will assume to collect metrics. The vendor serves many customers using the same platform. What should the role's trust policy require to prevent the vendor's platform from being tricked into acting on the wrong customer's role (the confused deputy problem)?$q$,$q$["A wildcard principal allowing any AWS account to assume the role","A unique ExternalId value in the trust policy condition, issued to only this customer","A permanent IAM user access key shared with the vendor","Removing MFA requirements to simplify vendor access"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A unique ExternalId condition ties the trust relationship to this specific customer, so even if the vendor's platform is manipulated into assuming a role on behalf of another tenant, it cannot succeed without that customer's own secret ExternalId. A wildcard principal, a shared long-term key, or dropping MFA all weaken the trust boundary instead of closing it.$q$),
('sc-q-503','awsscs',4,'4.1','multi',3,$q$Select the TWO approaches that let a workload obtain temporary, automatically rotated AWS credentials without storing a long-term access key. (Select two.)$q$,$q$["An EC2 instance profile role supplying credentials through the instance metadata service","IAM Roles Anywhere issuing role credentials to an on-premises server that presents an X.509 certificate","A shared IAM user access key embedded in the instance's startup script","A permanent access key stored in AWS Secrets Manager and rotated manually every 90 days"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$An instance profile role and IAM Roles Anywhere both hand out short-lived, automatically rotated credentials without ever creating a long-term key - one for workloads running on EC2, the other for workloads outside AWS. Embedding a shared user's key in a startup script or storing a permanent key in Secrets Manager still relies on a long-term secret that must be manually managed and can leak.$q$),
('sc-q-504','awsscs',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$A security team wants to require phishing-resistant multi-factor authentication for the root user and all administrator IAM identities. Which MFA device type provides phishing resistance that a virtual/software TOTP authenticator app does not?$q$,$q$["A virtual MFA app generating six-digit codes","A FIDO2/WebAuthn hardware security key","Email delivery of a one-time code","No MFA, relying on a strong password alone"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$FIDO2/WebAuthn hardware security keys cryptographically bind the authentication to the legitimate site's origin, so a phishing site cannot replay or relay the credential - a property TOTP codes (virtual or hardware) lack, since a phished code can be typed into any site. Email codes are not a supported AWS MFA factor and are themselves phishable; relying on a password alone provides no second factor.$q$),
('sc-q-505','awsscs',4,'4.1','ordering',2,$q$Order the steps to federate a workforce into IAM Identity Center from an external IdP such as Okta with SCIM auto-provisioning.$q$,$q$["Configure Okta as a SAML 2.0 identity source in IAM Identity Center","Enable SCIM synchronization so user and group changes propagate automatically","Create permission sets that define the AWS permissions for each synced group","Assign the permission sets to AWS accounts for the synced groups"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$You first connect the external IdP as the identity source, then turn on SCIM so identity changes flow in automatically, then define what access looks like via permission sets, and finally assign those permission sets to the accounts each group should reach.$q$),
('sc-q-506','awsscs',4,'4.1','mcq',3,$q$An IAM user wants temporary credentials that enforce MFA for their own existing identity and permissions, with no change of role, for use with the AWS CLI. Which STS API should they call?$q$,$q$["GetSessionToken, supplying their MFA device serial number and code","AssumeRole, targeting a role with identical permissions","AssumeRoleWithSAML","AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$GetSessionToken issues temporary credentials for the calling IAM user (or root) itself, and supplying an MFA serial and code lets those credentials satisfy aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent conditions - with no role switch involved. AssumeRole changes to a different role's identity and permissions, and the AssumeRoleWithSAML/WebIdentity calls are for external federation, not an existing IAM user's own session.$q$),
('sc-q-507','awsscs',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Several support engineers all assume the same shared "SupportRole" and then chain into further roles to complete tasks. Security wants every downstream CloudTrail event in the chain to show which individual engineer originally started it. What should the trust policy require?$q$,$q$["An sts:SourceIdentity condition, with each engineer setting their own source identity at the first AssumeRole call","Disabling role chaining across the organization","Creating a brand-new CloudTrail trail for support activity","Requiring each engineer to use a personal IAM user instead of any role"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Requiring and setting a source identity on the first AssumeRole call causes IAM to propagate that identity through every subsequent role in the chain, so CloudTrail events downstream still show the originating engineer even though the role's session name may change. Disabling chaining or adding another trail don't preserve individual attribution, and reverting to personal IAM users abandons the benefits of role-based access entirely.$q$),
('sc-q-508','awsscs',4,'4.1','mcq',1,$q$In AWS IAM Identity Center, what defines the specific set of AWS permissions that an assigned group of users receives once they sign in to a target account?$q$,$q$["A permission set","A Service Control Policy","A permissions boundary","An S3 bucket policy"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A permission set is a template of IAM policies that IAM Identity Center provisions as an IAM role in the target account for the assigned users or groups - it is what actually defines their access there. SCPs and permissions boundaries only cap the maximum allowable permissions rather than define what a group receives, and a bucket policy grants access to one resource, not an account-wide permission set.$q$),
('sc-q-509','awsscs',4,'4.1','matching',3,$q$Match each authentication mechanism to its primary use case.$q$,$q${"left":["AssumeRoleWithSAML","AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity","GetSessionToken","IAM Roles Anywhere"],"right":["Federating a corporate workforce through an on-premises SAML 2.0 identity provider","Federating a mobile or web app's end users through a public OIDC identity provider","Adding MFA to an existing IAM user's own temporary session with no role change","Issuing temporary role credentials to an on-premises server presenting an X.509 certificate"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each mechanism federates a different kind of caller into temporary AWS credentials: AssumeRoleWithSAML for enterprise SAML workforce federation, AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity for OIDC-based app federation, GetSessionToken for an existing IAM user adding MFA to their own session, and IAM Roles Anywhere for certificate-based access from outside AWS.$q$),
('sc-q-510','awsscs',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$A developer with no administrator permissions is able to launch an EC2 instance with a highly privileged IAM role attached, effectively escalating their own access. Which permission, if scoped to only a small set of approved role ARNs, would have prevented this?$q$,$q$["iam:PassRole","ec2:RunInstances","iam:CreateRole","sts:AssumeRole"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$iam:PassRole controls which IAM roles a principal is allowed to hand off to a service when creating a resource, such as attaching a role to an EC2 instance; scoping it to specific role ARNs stops a user from attaching a role more privileged than their own. ec2:RunInstances is needed to launch the instance at all but doesn't govern which role can be attached; iam:CreateRole only controls making new roles, and sts:AssumeRole governs assuming a role directly, not passing one to a service.$q$),
('sc-q-511','awsscs',4,'4.2','mcq',1,$q$An S3 bucket must invoke a Lambda function automatically whenever a new object is uploaded. What has to be attached to the Lambda function to permit S3 to invoke it?$q$,$q$["A resource-based (Lambda function) policy granting the S3 service principal invoke permission","A permissions boundary on the function","A Service Control Policy on the account","The Lambda execution role's identity-based policy"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A resource-based policy on the Lambda function itself grants the s3.amazonaws.com service principal permission to invoke it for that bucket - authorizing who may call in. A permissions boundary and SCP only cap maximum permissions rather than grant invoke access, and the function's execution role controls what the function itself can call outward, not who is allowed to trigger it.$q$),
('sc-q-512','awsscs',4,'4.2','mcq',3,$q$An OU in AWS Organizations has an SCP that explicitly denies iam:CreateUser for every account beneath it. Inside one of those accounts, an administrator has an identity policy explicitly granting iam:CreateUser. Can that administrator create an IAM user?$q$,$q$["No - the OU-level SCP's explicit deny is inherited by every account beneath it and cannot be overridden by an account-level allow","Yes - identity policies always take precedence over SCPs","Yes - SCPs only apply to the OU itself, not to accounts inside it","It depends on which one was created more recently"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SCPs are inherited down the OU hierarchy and intersected with every account's own policies; an explicit deny at any level in that chain - even a parent OU far above the account - always wins over an allow anywhere else, including a permissive identity policy inside the account. SCPs apply to every account under the OU, not just the OU itself, and evaluation order never depends on recency.$q$),
('sc-q-513','awsscs',4,'4.2','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements that correctly describe capabilities of IAM Access Analyzer. (Select two.)$q$,$q$["It can validate a policy document for syntax errors and security warnings before it is ever deployed","It can be scoped to an entire AWS organization to analyze external access across every member account centrally","It automatically removes public access from any S3 bucket it flags","It enforces multi-factor authentication on any role it finds shared externally"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Access Analyzer's policy validation checks a policy for syntax problems and security warnings (like overly broad wildcards) at authoring time, and an organization-scoped analyzer can centrally evaluate external access findings across every account in the org. It is analysis-only - it does not remediate flagged buckets itself or enforce MFA; both remain actions an operator or a separate automation must take based on its findings.$q$),
('sc-q-514','awsscs',4,'4.2','mcq',1,$q$A company wants an S3 bucket to be reachable only through one specific VPC endpoint, denying any request that arrives by another path even if the source IP address is inside the VPC's own address range. Which condition key enforces this in the bucket policy?$q$,$q$["aws:SourceVpce","aws:SourceIp","aws:PrincipalOrgID","aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$aws:SourceVpce restricts access to requests that traversed a specific VPC endpoint, which is tighter than filtering by source IP address since it doesn't matter where inside (or outside) the VPC the request's IP originated - only whether it went through that endpoint. aws:SourceIp filters by address range instead of endpoint, aws:PrincipalOrgID restricts by organization membership, and aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent checks for MFA, none of which pin traffic to one endpoint.$q$),
('sc-q-515','awsscs',4,'4.2','matching',1,$q$Match each authorization concept to what it actually does.$q$,$q${"left":["Service Control Policy (SCP)","Permissions boundary","Resource-based policy","iam:PassRole permission"],"right":["Caps the maximum permissions of every account and OU beneath it in AWS Organizations","Caps the maximum permissions a single IAM identity can ever be granted","Grants access by attaching directly to the resource itself, such as an S3 bucket policy","Controls which IAM roles a principal may hand off to an AWS service when creating a resource"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$These four authorization tools operate at different scopes: an SCP is an org-wide ceiling on accounts/OUs, a permissions boundary is a ceiling on one identity, a resource-based policy grants access from the resource's side (useful for cross-account sharing), and iam:PassRole specifically gates which roles can be attached to a resource being created - a common privilege-escalation choke point.$q$);
